-- Fixes a rotationIndex collision: an admin-added question (qmtkllqpx7nv,
-- diabetic neuropathy, Clinical Pain) already occupied rotationIndex 54 live,
-- which was never reflected in questions.json when migration_007 assigned
-- rotationIndex 54 to q_sba_morphine_breastfeeding_001 too. This moves the
-- latter to rotationIndex 70 (the next free slot) so Question-of-the-Day
-- rotation has no collision. Idempotent; safe to re-run.

update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":70,"question":"A 29-year-old woman who is exclusively breastfeeding her infant is recovering from surgery and requires ongoing analgesia. The team is considering the safety of various analgesic options during lactation. Which of the following analgesics carries the greatest concern regarding safety in breastfeeding?","choices":["Morphine","Paracetamol","Ibuprofen","Bupivacaine","Clonidine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<p>Analgesic choice in breastfeeding mothers must balance maternal pain control against the risk of drug transfer into breast milk and subsequent neonatal exposure, particularly for agents with active metabolites, sedative effects, or accumulation risk in the still-immature neonatal system.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Correct.</strong> Morphine (and opioids generally) is the analgesic of greatest concern in breastfeeding. While it can be given for breastfeeding women, caution is advised as morphine and its active metabolite (morphine-6-glucuronide) pass into breast milk, and neonates — particularly those under 1 month old — have reduced clearance capacity, increasing the risk of accumulation causing sedation and respiratory depression. Repeated or high-dose maternal opioid use warrants caution and neonatal monitoring; this risk is even more pronounced with codeine in ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolisers, but morphine itself still carries meaningful concern, especially in a very young infant.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Paracetamol is considered safe in breastfeeding; only minimal amounts are transferred into breast milk, and it lacks significant sedative or respiratory depressant effects on the infant.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Ibuprofen is considered the NSAID of choice in breastfeeding due to very low milk transfer, a short half-life, and no significant active metabolites of concern.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Bupivacaine is highly protein-bound with minimal systemic absorption and negligible transfer into breast milk when used regionally (e.g. wound catheter, epidural, nerve block); considered safe in breastfeeding.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Clonidine can pass into breast milk, and there is some caution advised, but it is generally considered compatible with breastfeeding at low maternal doses with monitoring; it carries substantially less concern than systemic opioids in this context.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Pharmacology Knowledge Summary</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Morphine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Opioid agonist (mu-receptor)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic via glucuronidation → active metabolites morphine-6-glucuronide (analgesic, can accumulate in renal impairment) and morphine-3-glucuronide (neuroexcitatory, non-analgesic)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Present in breast milk in clinically relevant amounts; relative infant dose is low-moderate but neonatal clearance is immature, especially &lt;1 month</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Neonatal sedation and respiratory depression, particularly with repeated dosing or in preterm/very young infants</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Practical note</strong></td><td>Short-course, lowest effective dose preferred; monitor infant for sedation, poor feeding, reduced tone</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Paracetamol</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Non-opioid analgesic/antipyretic; exact mechanism incompletely understood — central COX-3 inhibition. May act on the endocannabinoid system via the metabolite AM404, which inhibits reuptake of anandamide, and via descending serotonergic pathways</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Rapid absorption; bioavailability 65–85%; protein binding 25%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic glucuronidation/sulfation; toxic metabolite NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione (relevant in overdose)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Minimal; considered first-line analgesic in breastfeeding</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Essentially none at therapeutic doses; standard hepatotoxicity risk in maternal overdose only</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Ibuprofen</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Non-selective NSAID (COX-1/COX-2 inhibition)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Highly protein-bound, short half-life (~2 hours)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Very low (&lt;1% of maternal dose); preferred NSAID in lactation by most authorities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Standard NSAID risks apply to the mother (GI, renal, cardiovascular); negligible infant exposure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Caution</strong></td><td>Avoid in infants with (or at risk of) reduced renal function; general NSAID caveats around platelet function/bleeding apply to the mother</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Bupivacaine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Amide local anaesthetic (sodium channel blocker)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Highly protein-bound (~95%); hepatic metabolism via amide hydrolysis (hepatic amidases)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Minimal systemic absorption with regional techniques (wound catheters, epidural, peripheral nerve blocks); negligible milk transfer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Primarily systemic toxicity (cardiotoxicity/CNS toxicity) at high plasma levels from the maternal procedure itself, not from breastfeeding exposure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Practical note</strong></td><td>Considered compatible with breastfeeding when used as intended for regional analgesia</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clonidine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonist</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Uses</strong></td><td>Analgesic adjunct, sedation, and autonomic symptom control (e.g. opioid withdrawal, ADHD)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Detectable in breast milk; some case reports of neonatal sedation/hypotension with higher maternal doses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Sedation, hypotension, bradycardia in the infant, though generally regarded as compatible with breastfeeding at low doses with monitoring</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Practical note</strong></td><td>Caution advised with maternal doses higher than typical low-dose adjunct use; monitor infant alertness/feeding</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_sba_morphine_breastfeeding_001';
